6. Set aside the tax on what you take out, and pay it with your return in April or in four estimated payments during the year.
   (The money you take out has to cover the tax on it too; the plan’s withdrawals include it.)
7. Once a year, move every invested account to that year’s mix (cash stays cash): 47% stocks / 53% bonds this year, then about 3 percentage points a year from stocks into bonds through 2056, and 30% stocks / 70% bonds from then on.
   (This follows Vanguard’s Target Retirement path, tied to the year you turn 65 (2049). A target-date fund dated near 2049, the year you turn 65, follows about this path and makes these moves for you; with separate stock and bond funds, you make them yourself. Mix by year (stocks / bonds): 2051: 44 / 56 · 2052: 41 / 59 · 2053: 39 / 61 · 2054: 36 / 64 · 2055: 33 / 67 · 2056: 30 / 70. The results assume that year’s mix in every account. Each move sells some of what the brokerage account holds; the results already count the capital-gains tax on that sale in the year’s tax bill, so set it aside with the rest of the tax. Inside the 401(k)/IRA and Roth, moving costs no tax. Drifting toward more stocks raises the risk in a bad market; toward less lowers growth.)
8. Once a year, enter your real balances here and recalculate.
   (Markets will not follow any single simulated path. If the chance your money lasts falls, spending is the lever to adjust early.)
