exports[`the playbook for fourteen household profiles > C. High income, $2.4M pre-tax, retiring at 50 and 48 with an HSA 1`] = `
"traditional, retire 2034

## 2027 · Keep shifting the asset mix (You 43 · Spouse 41)
1. Each year until you retire, move every invested account to that year’s mix (cash stays cash): 86% stocks / 14% bonds in 2027, then about 1.5 percentage points a year from stocks into bonds, to 75% stocks / 25% bonds in 2034, the year you retire.
   (The shift is already under way: the results assume your accounts hold 87% stocks / 13% bonds in 2026, the plan’s first year. This follows Vanguard’s Target Retirement path, tied to the year you turn 65 (2049). A target-date fund dated near 2049, the year you turn 65, follows about this path and makes these moves for you; with separate stock and bond funds, you make them yourself. The results assume that year’s mix in every account each year. Each move sells some of what the brokerage account holds; the results already count the capital-gains tax on that sale, paid from the brokerage account itself. Inside the 401(k)/IRA, Roth and HSA, moving costs no tax.)

## 2034 · You retire (You 50 · Spouse 48)
1. Live on about $153,000 a year plus healthcare, in today’s dollars.
   (This is the budget the results are based on. Raise it with inflation each year: every figure here is in today’s dollars. Spending more than this, especially in the first years or in a bad market, is what runs money out early.)
2. Pay medical bills from the HSA before any other account.
   (That money is never taxed when it goes to healthcare, and no other account works that way.)
3. Buy your own health insurance until each of you turns 65, when Medicare takes over.
   (The plan budgets about $36,000 a year for it in 2034, in today’s dollars (it rises 1.5% a year faster than prices, so look at this again each year), at full price with no subsidy, on top of your regular spending.)
4. Each January, take money out of the 401(k)/IRA up to the top of the 10% tax bracket.
   (This money is taxed whenever it comes out. Taking some every year while your income is low means it is taxed at the lowest rate, instead of piling up and being taxed at a higher rate later. For 2026 the top of the bracket is about $57,000 of income for a married couple (the $32,200 standard deduction plus the $24,800 bracket), minus any other income you already expect that year, such as a pension, interest or the taxable part of Social Security. Look the numbers up each year; the IRS raises them with inflation. Start with your account, the older of you, because it opens up penalty-free first; then Spouse’s.)
5. Don’t spend it while you are both under 59½: move it straight into a Roth IRA and write down the year.
   (Ask the brokerage for a “Roth conversion”. Spending that money would cost a 10% penalty; converting it costs only the income tax, and after 5 years it can be spent with no tax or penalty at all.)
6. For everything else you need this year, take money in this order, moving to the next account only when the one before is empty (the HSA stays for medical bills):
   a. Cash.
   b. Brokerage. You pay tax only on the growth, at the low capital-gains rate.
   c. Roth: only what you put in yourself, and conversions that are 5 or more years old. The growth and newer conversions stay put.
   d. If those run out while you are under 59½, the 401(k)/IRA is next, then Roth growth, both with the 10% penalty. The results above count how often that happens. (The IRS has exceptions, such as the Rule of 55, that the plan doesn’t count on.)
   (Cash and brokerage cost the least tax to spend. Roth money grows tax-free, so it is worth the most if it is left alone the longest.)
7. Set aside the tax on what you take out, and pay it with your return in April or in four estimated payments during the year.
   (The money you take out has to cover the tax on it too; the plan’s withdrawals include it.)
8. Once a year, move every invested account to that year’s mix (cash stays cash): 75% stocks / 25% bonds this year, then about 1.5 to 3 percentage points a year from stocks into bonds through 2056, and 30% stocks / 70% bonds from then on.
   (This follows Vanguard’s Target Retirement path, tied to the year you turn 65 (2049). A target-date fund dated near 2049, the year you turn 65, follows about this path and makes these moves for you; with separate stock and bond funds, you make them yourself. Mix by year (stocks / bonds): 2035: 74 / 26 · 2036: 72 / 28 · 2037: 71 / 29 · 2038: 69 / 31 · 2039: 68 / 32 · … · 2056: 30 / 70. The results assume that year’s mix in every account. Each move sells some of what the brokerage account holds; the results already count the capital-gains tax on that sale in the year’s tax bill, so set it aside with the rest of the tax. Inside the 401(k)/IRA, Roth and HSA, moving costs no tax. Drifting toward more stocks raises the risk in a bad market; toward less lowers growth.)
9. Once a year, enter your real balances here and recalculate.
   (Markets will not follow any single simulated path. If the chance your money lasts falls, spending is the lever to adjust early.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2034: spent $189K; $36K from the HSA; $99K from cash; $61K from brokerage; $36K moved from 401(k)/IRA to Roth; $8K in taxes.

## 2044 · You turn 60 (You 60 · Spouse 58)
1. Your 401(k)/IRA and all of your Roth can now be used with no penalty.
   (The IRS rule is 59½; the plan counts the year you turn 60.)
2. From now on, spend what you need from the yearly withdrawal and move only the leftover into Roth. In the order above, your 401(k)/IRA now comes after brokerage and before Roth.
   (Before 59½, spending that money cost a 10% penalty. Now it doesn’t, so it is cheaper to spend than Roth money, which keeps growing tax-free.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2044: spent $195K; $168K from brokerage; $39K from 401(k)/IRA; $12K in taxes.

## 2046 · Spouse turns 60 (You 62 · Spouse 60)
1. Spouse’s 401(k)/IRA and all of Spouse’s Roth can now be used with no penalty.
   (The IRS rule is 59½; the plan counts the year Spouse turns 60.)
2. From now on, spend what you need from the yearly withdrawal and move only the leftover into Roth. In the order above, Spouse’s 401(k)/IRA now comes after brokerage and before Roth.
   (Before 59½, spending that money cost a 10% penalty. Now it doesn’t, so it is cheaper to spend than Roth money, which keeps growing tax-free.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2046: spent $196K; $178K from brokerage; $27K from 401(k)/IRA; $9K in taxes.

## 2049 · You turn 65 (You 65 · Spouse 63)
1. You move to Medicare. Sign up in the 3 months before the birthday.
   (Signing up late carries a lifelong penalty. Health insurance for you goes from about $22,500 to about $10,600 a year in 2049, in today’s dollars.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2049: spent $186K; $146K from brokerage; $45K from 401(k)/IRA; $5K in taxes.

## 2051 · You start Social Security (age 67) · Spouse turns 65 (You 67 · Spouse 65)
1. Apply for Social Security up to 4 months before you turn 67.
   (From then on your benefit adds about $33K a year in today’s dollars, so you take that much less from the accounts. It also counts as income, so there is less room under the 10% bracket and the yearly 401(k)/IRA withdrawal shrinks. The amount already assumes only 73% of the full benefit is paid by then, in case Congress does not fix the shortfall in Social Security’s trust fund. If it does, you will get more.)
2. Spouse moves to Medicare. Sign up in the 3 months before the birthday.
   (Signing up late carries a lifelong penalty. Health insurance for Spouse goes from about $23,200 to about $10,900 a year in 2051, in today’s dollars.)
3. HSA money can now also be spent on anything else.
   (Once you are both 65 it is taxed like 401(k)/IRA money, with no penalty. The plan still uses it for medical bills first.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2051: spent $175K; $17K from Social Security; $193K from 401(k)/IRA; $35K in taxes.

## 2053 · Spouse starts Social Security (age 67) (You 69 · Spouse 67)
1. Apply for Social Security up to 4 months before Spouse turns 67.
