exports[`the playbook for fourteen household profiles > I. Ten-year age gap, 62 and 52 at retirement, with an HSA 1`] = `
"traditional, retire 2034

## 2027 · Keep shifting the asset mix (You 55 · Spouse 45)
1. Each year until you retire, move every invested account to that year’s mix (cash stays cash): 68% stocks / 32% bonds in 2027, then about 1.5 to 2 percentage points a year from stocks into bonds, to 56% stocks / 44% bonds in 2034, the year you retire.
   (The shift is already under way: the results assume your accounts hold 69% stocks / 31% bonds in 2026, the plan’s first year. This follows Vanguard’s Target Retirement path, tied to the year you turn 65 (2037). A target-date fund dated near 2037, the year you turn 65, follows about this path and makes these moves for you; with separate stock and bond funds, you make them yourself. The results assume that year’s mix in every account each year. Each move sells some of what the brokerage account holds; the results already count the capital-gains tax on that sale, paid from the brokerage account itself. Inside the 401(k)/IRA, Roth and HSA, moving costs no tax.)

## 2034 · You retire (You 62 · Spouse 52)
1. Live on about $76,500 a year plus healthcare, in today’s dollars.
   (This is the budget the results are based on. Raise it with inflation each year: every figure here is in today’s dollars. Spending more than this, especially in the first years or in a bad market, is what runs money out early.)
2. Pay medical bills from the HSA before any other account.
   (That money is never taxed when it goes to healthcare, and no other account works that way.)
3. Buy your own health insurance until each of you turns 65, when Medicare takes over.
   (The plan budgets about $36,000 a year for it in 2034, in today’s dollars (it rises 1.5% a year faster than prices, so look at this again each year), at full price with no subsidy, on top of your regular spending.)
4. Each January, take money out of the 401(k)/IRA up to the top of the 10% tax bracket.
   (This money is taxed whenever it comes out. Taking some every year while your income is low means it is taxed at the lowest rate, instead of piling up and being taxed at a higher rate later. For 2026 the top of the bracket is about $57,000 of income for a married couple (the $32,200 standard deduction plus the $24,800 bracket), minus any other income you already expect that year, such as a pension, interest or the taxable part of Social Security. Look the numbers up each year; the IRS raises them with inflation. Start with your account, the older of you, because it opens up penalty-free first; then Spouse’s.)
5. Don’t spend the part taken from Spouse’s 401(k)/IRA while Spouse is under 59½: move it straight into Spouse’s Roth IRA and write down the year. Spend what you need from your withdrawal and convert only the leftover.
   (Ask the brokerage for a “Roth conversion”. Spending that money would cost a 10% penalty; converting it costs only the income tax, and after 5 years it can be spent with no tax or penalty at all.)
6. For everything else you need this year, take money in this order, moving to the next account only when the one before is empty (the HSA stays for medical bills):
   a. Cash.
   b. Brokerage. You pay tax only on the growth, at the low capital-gains rate.
   c. Your 401(k)/IRA, beyond the yearly amount above.
   d. Roth: only what you put in yourself, and conversions that are 5 or more years old. The growth and newer conversions stay put.
   e. If those run out while Spouse is under 59½, Spouse’s 401(k)/IRA is next, then Roth growth, both with the 10% penalty. The results above count how often that happens. (The IRS has exceptions, such as the Rule of 55, that the plan doesn’t count on.)
   (Cash and brokerage cost the least tax to spend. Roth money grows tax-free, so it is worth the most if it is left alone the longest.)
7. Set aside the tax on what you take out, and pay it with your return in April or in four estimated payments during the year.
   (The money you take out has to cover the tax on it too; the plan’s withdrawals include it.)
8. Once a year, move every invested account to that year’s mix (cash stays cash): 56% stocks / 44% bonds this year, then about 2 to 3 percentage points a year from stocks into bonds through 2044, and 30% stocks / 70% bonds from then on.
   (This follows Vanguard’s Target Retirement path, tied to the year you turn 65 (2037). A target-date fund dated near 2037, the year you turn 65, follows about this path and makes these moves for you; with separate stock and bond funds, you make them yourself. Mix by year (stocks / bonds): 2035: 54 / 46 · 2036: 52 / 48 · 2037: 50 / 50 · 2038: 47 / 53 · 2039: 44 / 56 · … · 2044: 30 / 70. The results assume that year’s mix in every account. Each move sells some of what the brokerage account holds; the results already count the capital-gains tax on that sale in the year’s tax bill, so set it aside with the rest of the tax. Inside the 401(k)/IRA, Roth and HSA, moving costs no tax. Drifting toward more stocks raises the risk in a bad market; toward less lowers growth.)
9. Once a year, enter your real balances here and recalculate.
   (Markets will not follow any single simulated path. If the chance your money lasts falls, spending is the lever to adjust early.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2034: spent $113K; $36K from the HSA; $29K from cash; $523 from brokerage; $51K from 401(k)/IRA; $4K in taxes.

## 2037 · You turn 65 (You 65 · Spouse 55)
1. You move to Medicare. Sign up in the 3 months before the birthday.
   (Signing up late carries a lifelong penalty. Health insurance for you goes from about $18,800 to about $8,800 a year in 2037, in today’s dollars.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2037: spent $104K; $27K from the HSA; $31K from brokerage; $51K from 401(k)/IRA; $5K in taxes.

## 2039 · You start Social Security (age 67) (You 67 · Spouse 57)
1. Apply for Social Security up to 4 months before you turn 67.
   (From then on your benefit adds about $23K a year in today’s dollars, so you take that much less from the accounts. It also counts as income, so there is less room under the 10% bracket and the yearly 401(k)/IRA withdrawal shrinks. The amount already assumes only 76% of the full benefit is paid by then, in case Congress does not fix the shortfall in Social Security’s trust fund. If it does, you will get more.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2039: spent $105K; $11K from Social Security; $56K from brokerage; $42K from 401(k)/IRA; $4K in taxes.

