1. Spouse’s 401(k)/IRA and all of Spouse’s Roth can now be used with no penalty.
   (The IRS rule is 59½; the plan counts the year Spouse turns 60.)
2. From now on, spend what you need from the yearly withdrawal and move only the leftover into Roth. In the order above, Spouse’s 401(k)/IRA now comes after brokerage and before Roth.
   (Before 59½, spending that money cost a 10% penalty. Now it doesn’t, so it is cheaper to spend than Roth money, which keeps growing tax-free.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2042: spent $106K; $23K from Social Security; $53K from brokerage; $34K from 401(k)/IRA; $4K in taxes.

## 2044 · Last change to the asset mix (You 72 · Spouse 62)
1. Move every invested account to 30% stocks / 70% bonds this year, the last step of the shift (cash stays cash). From now on, put every invested account back to that mix once a year.
   (This is where Vanguard’s Target Retirement path ends, 7 years after you turn 65. A target-date fund dated near 2037, the year you turn 65, follows about this path and makes these moves for you; with separate stock and bond funds, you make them yourself. The results assume this mix in every account, every year from now on. This move sells some of what the brokerage account holds; the results already count the capital-gains tax on that sale in this year’s tax bill, so set it aside with the rest of the tax. Inside the 401(k)/IRA, Roth and HSA, moving costs no tax.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2044: spent $107K; $23K from Social Security; $52K from brokerage; $37K from 401(k)/IRA; $4K in taxes.

## 2047 · You turn 75 · Spouse turns 65 (You 75 · Spouse 65)
1. Take the required minimum withdrawal from your 401(k)/IRA every year before December 31. Spend it first; whatever you don’t need goes into the brokerage account.
   (The IRS now requires it (a “required minimum distribution”, or RMD): about 4% of the balance at first and a slightly bigger share each year, from an IRS table. Your brokerage will tell you the exact figure. Missing it costs a 25% penalty.)
2. Spouse moves to Medicare. Sign up in the 3 months before the birthday.
   (Signing up late carries a lifelong penalty. Health insurance for Spouse goes from about $21,900 to about $10,300 a year in 2047, in today’s dollars.)
3. HSA money can now also be spent on anything else.
   (Once you are both 65 it is taxed like 401(k)/IRA money, with no penalty. The plan still uses it for medical bills first.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2047: spent $97K; $22K from Social Security; $85K from 401(k)/IRA; $28K of that required by the IRS; $10K in taxes.

## 2049 · Spouse starts Social Security (age 67) (You 77 · Spouse 67)
1. Apply for Social Security up to 4 months before Spouse turns 67.
   (From then on Spouse’s benefit adds about $22K a year in today’s dollars, so you take that much less from the accounts. It also counts as income, so there is less room under the 10% bracket and the yearly 401(k)/IRA withdrawal shrinks. The amount already assumes only 74% of the full benefit is paid by then, in case Congress does not fix the shortfall in Social Security’s trust fund. If it does, you will get more.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2049: spent $98K; $33K from Social Security; $74K from 401(k)/IRA; $28K of that required by the IRS; $9K in taxes.

## 2057 · Spouse turns 75 (You 85 · Spouse 75)
1. Take the required minimum withdrawal from Spouse’s 401(k)/IRA every year before December 31. Spend it first; whatever you don’t need goes into the brokerage account.
   (The IRS now requires it (a “required minimum distribution”, or RMD): about 4% of the balance at first and a slightly bigger share each year, from an IRS table. Your brokerage will tell you the exact figure. Missing it costs a 25% penalty.)
