8. Once a year, move every invested account to that year’s mix (cash stays cash): 70% stocks / 25% bonds / 5% cash this year, then about 3 percentage points a year from stocks into bonds and cash from 2044 through 2053, and 40% stocks / 50% bonds / 10% cash from then on.
   (This is the path you entered: 70% stocks / 25% bonds / 5% cash at first, moving to 40% stocks / 50% bonds / 10% cash in equal steps, one in each year you are 60 to 69. Mix by year (stocks / bonds / cash): 2044: 67 / 28 / 5 · 2045: 64 / 30 / 6 · 2046: 61 / 33 / 6 · 2047: 58 / 35 / 7 · 2048: 55 / 38 / 7 · … · 2053: 40 / 50 / 10. The results assume that year’s mix in every account. Each move sells some of what the brokerage account holds; the results already count the capital-gains tax on that sale in the year’s tax bill, so set it aside with the rest of the tax. Inside the 401(k)/IRA and Roth, moving costs no tax. Drifting toward more stocks raises the risk in a bad market; toward less lowers growth.)
