## 2051 · You turn 65 (You 65 · Spouse 71)
1. You move to Medicare. Sign up in the 3 months before the birthday.
   (Signing up late carries a lifelong penalty. Health insurance for you goes from about $23,200 to about $10,900 a year in 2051, in today’s dollars.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2051: spent $98K; $22K from Social Security; $50K from brokerage; $31K from 401(k)/IRA; $4K in taxes.

## 2053 · You start Social Security (age 67) (You 67 · Spouse 73)
1. Apply for Social Security up to 4 months before you turn 67.
   (From then on your benefit adds about $22K a year in today’s dollars, so you take that much less from the accounts. It also counts as income, so there is less room under the 10% bracket and the yearly 401(k)/IRA withdrawal shrinks. The amount already assumes only 73% of the full benefit is paid by then, in case Congress does not fix the shortfall in Social Security’s trust fund. If it does, you will get more.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2053: spent $99K; $33K from Social Security; $47K from brokerage; $23K from 401(k)/IRA; $4K in taxes.

## 2055 · Spouse turns 75 (You 69 · Spouse 75)
1. Take the required minimum withdrawal from Spouse’s 401(k)/IRA every year before December 31. Spend it first; whatever you don’t need goes into the brokerage account.
   (The IRS now requires it (a “required minimum distribution”, or RMD): about 4% of the balance at first and a slightly bigger share each year, from an IRS table. Your brokerage will tell you the exact figure. Missing it costs a 25% penalty.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2055: spent $100K; $44K from Social Security; $82K from 401(k)/IRA; $82K of that required by the IRS; $12K not needed and reinvested; $14K in taxes.

## 2058 · Last change to the asset mix (You 72 · Spouse 78)
1. Move every invested account to 30% stocks / 70% bonds this year, the last step of the shift (cash stays cash). From now on, put every invested account back to that mix once a year.
   (This is where Vanguard’s Target Retirement path ends, 7 years after you turn 65. A target-date fund dated near 2051, the year you turn 65, follows about this path and makes these moves for you; with separate stock and bond funds, you make them yourself. The results assume this mix in every account, every year from now on. This move sells some of what the brokerage account holds; the results already count the capital-gains tax on that sale in this year’s tax bill, so set it aside with the rest of the tax. Inside the 401(k)/IRA and Roth, moving costs no tax.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2058: spent $101K; $43K from Social Security; $79K from 401(k)/IRA; $79K of that required by the IRS; $7K not needed and reinvested; $14K in taxes.

## 2061 · You turn 75 (You 75 · Spouse 81)
1. Take the required minimum withdrawal from your 401(k)/IRA every year before December 31. Spend it first; whatever you don’t need goes into the brokerage account.
   (The IRS now requires it (a “required minimum distribution”, or RMD): about 4% of the balance at first and a slightly bigger share each year, from an IRS table. Your brokerage will tell you the exact figure. Missing it costs a 25% penalty.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2061: spent $102K; $43K from Social Security; $207K from 401(k)/IRA; $207K of that required by the IRS; $98K not needed and reinvested; $49K in taxes.
"
`;

exports[`the playbook for fourteen household profiles > N. Both born the same year, 57 at retirement, with an HSA 1`] = `
"traditional, retire 2041

## 2027 · Keep shifting the asset mix (You 43 · Spouse 43)
1. Each year until you retire, move every invested account to that year’s mix (cash stays cash): 86% stocks / 14% bonds in 2027, then about 1.5 percentage points a year from stocks into bonds, to 65% stocks / 35% bonds in 2041, the year you retire.
   (The shift is already under way: the results assume your accounts hold 87% stocks / 13% bonds in 2026, the plan’s first year. This follows Vanguard’s Target Retirement path, tied to the year you turn 65 (2049). A target-date fund dated near 2049, the year you turn 65, follows about this path and makes these moves for you; with separate stock and bond funds, you make them yourself. The results assume that year’s mix in every account each year. Each move sells some of what the brokerage account holds; the results already count the capital-gains tax on that sale, paid from the brokerage account itself. Inside the 401(k)/IRA, Roth and HSA, moving costs no tax.)

## 2041 · You retire (You 57 · Spouse 57)
1. Live on about $76,500 a year plus healthcare, in today’s dollars.
   (This is the budget the results are based on. Raise it with inflation each year: every figure here is in today’s dollars. Spending more than this, especially in the first years or in a bad market, is what runs money out early.)
2. Pay medical bills from the HSA before any other account.
   (That money is never taxed when it goes to healthcare, and no other account works that way.)
3. Buy your own health insurance until each of you turns 65, when Medicare takes over.
   (The plan budgets about $40,000 a year for it in 2041, in today’s dollars (it rises 1.5% a year faster than prices, so look at this again each year), at full price with no subsidy, on top of your regular spending.)
4. Each January, take money out of the 401(k)/IRA up to the top of the 10% tax bracket.
   (This money is taxed whenever it comes out. Taking some every year while your income is low means it is taxed at the lowest rate, instead of piling up and being taxed at a higher rate later. For 2026 the top of the bracket is about $57,000 of income for a married couple (the $32,200 standard deduction plus the $24,800 bracket), minus any other income you already expect that year, such as a pension, interest or the taxable part of Social Security. Look the numbers up each year; the IRS raises them with inflation. Start with your account; then Spouse’s.)
5. Don’t spend it while you are both under 59½: move it straight into a Roth IRA and write down the year.
   (Ask the brokerage for a “Roth conversion”. Spending that money would cost a 10% penalty; converting it costs only the income tax, and after 5 years it can be spent with no tax or penalty at all.)
6. For everything else you need this year, take money in this order, moving to the next account only when the one before is empty (the HSA stays for medical bills):
   a. Cash.
   b. Brokerage. You pay tax only on the growth, at the low capital-gains rate.
   c. Roth: only what you put in yourself, and conversions that are 5 or more years old. The growth and newer conversions stay put.
   d. If those run out while you are under 59½, the 401(k)/IRA is next, then Roth growth, both with the 10% penalty. The results above count how often that happens. (The IRS has exceptions, such as the Rule of 55, that the plan doesn’t count on.)
   (Cash and brokerage cost the least tax to spend. Roth money grows tax-free, so it is worth the most if it is left alone the longest.)
7. Set aside the tax on what you take out, and pay it with your return in April or in four estimated payments during the year.
   (The money you take out has to cover the tax on it too; the plan’s withdrawals include it.)
8. Once a year, move every invested account to that year’s mix (cash stays cash): 65% stocks / 35% bonds this year, then about 1.5 to 3 percentage points a year from stocks into bonds through 2056, and 30% stocks / 70% bonds from then on.
   (This follows Vanguard’s Target Retirement path, tied to the year you turn 65 (2049). A target-date fund dated near 2049, the year you turn 65, follows about this path and makes these moves for you; with separate stock and bond funds, you make them yourself. Mix by year (stocks / bonds): 2042: 63 / 37 · 2043: 62 / 38 · 2044: 60 / 40 · 2045: 58 / 42 · 2046: 56 / 44 · … · 2056: 30 / 70. The results assume that year’s mix in every account. Each move sells some of what the brokerage account holds; the results already count the capital-gains tax on that sale in the year’s tax bill, so set it aside with the rest of the tax. Inside the 401(k)/IRA, Roth and HSA, moving costs no tax. Drifting toward more stocks raises the risk in a bad market; toward less lowers growth.)
9. Once a year, enter your real balances here and recalculate.
   (Markets will not follow any single simulated path. If the chance your money lasts falls, spending is the lever to adjust early.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2041: spent $117K; $40K from the HSA; $46K from cash; $35K from brokerage; $46K moved from 401(k)/IRA to Roth; $4K in taxes.

## 2044 · You turn 60 · Spouse turns 60 (You 60 · Spouse 60)
1. Your 401(k)/IRA and all of your Roth can now be used with no penalty.
   (The IRS rule is 59½; the plan counts the year you turn 60.)
2. From now on, spend what you need from the yearly withdrawal and move only the leftover into Roth. In the order above, your 401(k)/IRA now comes after brokerage and before Roth.
   (Before 59½, spending that money cost a 10% penalty. Now it doesn’t, so it is cheaper to spend than Roth money, which keeps growing tax-free.)
3. Spouse’s 401(k)/IRA and all of Spouse’s Roth can now be used with no penalty.
   (The IRS rule is 59½; the plan counts the year Spouse turns 60.)
4. From now on, spend what you need from the yearly withdrawal and move only the leftover into Roth. In the order above, Spouse’s 401(k)/IRA now comes after brokerage and before Roth.
   (Before 59½, spending that money cost a 10% penalty. Now it doesn’t, so it is cheaper to spend than Roth money, which keeps growing tax-free.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2044: spent $118K; $75K from brokerage; $49K from 401(k)/IRA; $5K in taxes.

## 2049 · You turn 65 · Spouse turns 65 (You 65 · Spouse 65)
1. You move to Medicare. Sign up in the 3 months before the birthday.
   (Signing up late carries a lifelong penalty. Health insurance for you goes from about $22,500 to about $10,600 a year in 2049, in today’s dollars.)
2. Spouse moves to Medicare. Sign up in the 3 months before the birthday.
   (Signing up late carries a lifelong penalty. Health insurance for Spouse goes from about $22,500 to about $10,600 a year in 2049, in today’s dollars.)
3. HSA money can now also be spent on anything else.
   (Once you are both 65 it is taxed like 401(k)/IRA money, with no penalty. The plan still uses it for medical bills first.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2049: spent $98K; $48K from brokerage; $55K from 401(k)/IRA; $5K in taxes.

## 2051 · You start Social Security (age 67) · Spouse starts Social Security (age 67) (You 67 · Spouse 67)
1. Apply for Social Security up to 4 months before you turn 67.
   (From then on your benefit adds about $22K a year in today’s dollars, so you take that much less from the accounts. It also counts as income, so there is less room under the 10% bracket and the yearly 401(k)/IRA withdrawal shrinks. The amount already assumes only 73% of the full benefit is paid by then, in case Congress does not fix the shortfall in Social Security’s trust fund. If it does, you will get more.)
2. Apply for Social Security up to 4 months before Spouse turns 67.
   (From then on Spouse’s benefit adds about $22K a year in today’s dollars, so you take that much less from the accounts. It also counts as income, so there is less room under the 10% bracket and the yearly 401(k)/IRA withdrawal shrinks. The amount already assumes only 73% of the full benefit is paid by then, in case Congress does not fix the shortfall in Social Security’s trust fund. If it does, you will get more.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2051: spent $98K; $22K from Social Security; $41K from brokerage; $39K from 401(k)/IRA; $4K in taxes.

