1. Move every invested account to 30% stocks / 70% bonds this year, the last step of the shift (cash stays cash). From now on, put every invested account back to that mix once a year.
   (This is where Vanguard’s Target Retirement path ends, 7 years after you turn 65. A target-date fund dated near 2049, the year you turn 65, follows about this path and makes these moves for you; with separate stock and bond funds, you make them yourself. The results assume this mix in every account, every year from now on. This move sells some of what the brokerage account holds; the results already count the capital-gains tax on that sale in this year’s tax bill, so set it aside with the rest of the tax. Inside the 401(k)/IRA, Roth and HSA, moving costs no tax.)
> In the typical market of the table below, 2056: spent $100K; $43K from Social Security; $22K from brokerage; $38K from 401(k)/IRA; $3K in taxes.

